{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0040", "block": 40, "kind": "heading", "translate": true, "chars": 20, "sha256": "9933273acddf32b9", "text": "Building an AI Virus"}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0041", "block": 41, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 320, "sha256": "920ccdb02a60a942", "text": "When seeing this, one will notice that this basically allows the creation of a virus. An attacker can embed instructions and once they gain code execution, additional malware can compromise other Git projects (and RAG sources) to embed the malicious instructions, and commit the changes or even force push them upstream."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0042", "block": 42, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 92, "sha256": "ef1dbefd2043e149", "text": "This can lead to further spread as other developers unknowingly propagate the infected code."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0043", "block": 43, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 63, "sha256": "2501b0ffd7e6db36", "text": "**Finally, we also need to talk about invisible instructions!**"}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0044", "block": 44, "kind": "heading", "translate": true, "chars": 28, "sha256": "262b3511fb4c2e0e", "text": "Using Invisible Instructions"}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0045", "block": 45, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 292, "sha256": "f36e49a59ae441d9", "text": "One might say that it would be quickly discovered if instructions are embedded as comments. So in order to make it a bit more interesting, I went ahead and created an invisible payload that achieves the attack chain, but is not visible to users. This was not as reliable, but it still worked:"}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0046", "block": 46, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 468, "sha256": "dad078ad5074a8c2", "text": "**Note:** Although the demo here with invisible instructions worked multiple times for me, using invisible instructions often leads to the exploit being very unreliable, and is also commonly also refused by the model and there is also typically a visual indicator that VS Code shows about Unicode characters. However, attacks (and models) get better over time. It’s also worth highlighting that not all models are vulnerable to such invisible prompt injection attacks."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0047", "block": 47, "kind": "heading", "translate": true, "chars": 23, "sha256": "ac1d34923f58782a", "text": "Recommendations and Fix"}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0048", "block": 48, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 434, "sha256": "d6d8987a6c05e100", "text": "There are actually more attack angles then just the YOLO mode example I shared. When Microsoft asked me if there is any more info I have, I had looked a bit more and noticed there are other problematic places, for instance `.vscode/tasks.json` that the AI can write to, or adding fake malicious MCP servers, etc which can lead to code execution. And the AI can reconfigure the user interface and configuration settings of the project."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0049", "block": 49, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 238, "sha256": "d4303813ec049176", "text": "**Recently I noticed that developers often use multiple agents, so there is also the threat of overwriting other agent configuration files (allow-list bash commands, add MCP servers…), as they are commonly in the project folder as well.**"}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0050", "block": 50, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 167, "sha256": "2f9be0135d6ea488", "text": "Ideally, the AI would not be able to modify files without a human first approving it. Many other editors do show the diff, which then can be approved by the developer."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0051", "block": 51, "kind": "heading", "translate": true, "chars": 22, "sha256": "23091849cfc0ee5c", "text": "Responsible Disclosure"}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0052", "block": 52, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 296, "sha256": "bcec7d974d106194", "text": "After reporting the vulnerability on June 29, 2025 Microsoft confirmed the repro and asked a few follow up questions. A few weeks later MSRC pointed out that it is an issue they were already tracking, and that it will be patched by August. With the August Patch Tuesday release this is now fixed."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0053", "block": 53, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 448, "sha256": "cb276a133eafadf8", "text": "Shout out to [Markus Vervier](https://x.com/marver) from [Persistent Security](https://persistent-security.net/) who has also identified and reported this vulnerability to Microsoft. You can find their write-up [here](https://www.persistent-security.net/post/part-iii-vscode-copilot-wormable-command-execution-via-prompt-injection). And also a shout out to [Ari Marzuk](https://x.com/Ari_MaccariTA) who seems to also have discovered it in parallel."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0054", "block": 54, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 88, "sha256": "a2967c971a74c06c", "text": "Thanks to the members of the MSRC and product team for the help in getting it mitigated."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0055", "block": 55, "kind": "heading", "translate": true, "chars": 10, "sha256": "89db2ad881f29c18", "text": "Conclusion"}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0056", "block": 56, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 270, "sha256": "80af9f59bbcdc506", "text": "This is another example of how an AI agent might not stay in its box! By modifying its own environment GitHub Copilot can escalate privileges and execute code to compromise the developer’s machine. It’s a not uncommon design flaw in agentic systems as I have discovered."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0057", "block": 57, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 93, "sha256": "97015b199fea0aaa", "text": "Keep looking out for such design flaws, these should be easily caught during threat modeling."}
{"slug": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio", "id": "embracethered-com-blog-posts-2025-github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injectio#b0058", "block": 58, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 7, "sha256": "80077954a8eaf31b", "text": "Cheers."}
